# pcre2 / pcre2-syntax 10.40-6.el9 in quay.io/quarkus/ubi9-quarkus-micro-image (el9, RHEL 9.8),
# CVE-2026-89161 (pcre2_jit_match incorrect free) and CVE-2026-86145 (pcre2_dfa_match out-of-bounds
# write with a recursive pattern or attacker-controlled regex). Upstream fixed both in 10.48; RHEL
# has shipped no fixed el9 package and marks CVE-2026-86145 "will not fix", so every micro base tag
# carries 10.40, including the 2026-09-27 build pinned here. The library is present only because the
# base image's libselinux requires it: the native binary links libz and libc alone (readelf NEEDED)
# and compiles its regular expressions from java.util.regex, so no PCRE2 code runs in the bot. The
# micro base has no package manager to apply an update, and the :*-distroless image does not ship
# pcre2. Remove when RHEL ships pcre2 >= 10.48 for el9.
CVE-2026-89161
CVE-2026-86145

# libssl3 3.0.20-1~deb12u2 in the debian12-based images (CVE-2026-14456): unbounded memory
# growth in the OpenSSL QUIC *server* listener. Per the upstream advisory the issue exists only
# since OpenSSL 3.5, where the QUIC server was added — the 3.0.x branch Debian 12 ships has no
# QUIC server code, and ThrillhouseBot runs no QUIC listener regardless. Debian has published no
# fixed package. Remove when Debian ships a libssl3 update that clears the tracker entry.
CVE-2026-14456

# libssl3 3.0.20-1~deb12u2 in the debian12-based images (CVE-2026-84782): out-of-bounds read in the
# DTLS handshake retransmission path. It needs a DTLS (datagram TLS) session; ThrillhouseBot speaks
# only TLS over TCP, and does that through the JVM's own JSSE implementation compiled into the native
# binary, which does not load libssl3 at all. Debian has published no fixed package. Remove when
# Debian ships a libssl3 update that clears the tracker entry.
CVE-2026-84782
